<VsLayout slug="praktika">
  <p>In one line, my Praktika review: 4.7 stars on the App Store, 3.6 on Trustpilot, and a good guided conversation course for beginners that's weak at keeping your vocabulary, and with no per-sound accent history that reviewers mention.</p>

  <p>Praktika has more languages, avatars and a lower price. Vocele has a learning system around the conversation that Praktika doesn't: spaced-repetition vocabulary, stories you answer aloud, pronunciation scored word by word and tracked over weeks, corrections drawn inside the sentence you said, and reports of sentences where you could have used each grammar rule. Pick Praktika for breadth and a face, and Vocele for words that stick and an accent you can measure.</p>

  <p>Before designing Vocele I collected 46 screenshots of Praktika's onboarding, chat and paywall as design references, and I read <a href="https://languatalk.com/blog/praktika-review/">LanguaTalk's review</a> and <a href="https://practiceme.app/vs/praktika">PracticeMe's comparison page</a> (PracticeMe is another speaking app). After that, I think Praktika has the most lifelike avatars I've seen in an AI tutor. This page is written without a grudge.</p>

  <h2>Praktika review: what users and reviewers say</h2>

  <p>The ratings split depending on where you look. On the <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/praktika-ai-language-tutor/id1624701477">US App Store listing</a> Praktika holds 4.7 stars from about 165,000 ratings. On <a href="https://www.trustpilot.com/review/praktika.ai">Trustpilot</a> it has 3.6 from 1,263 reviews, and that average hides a split, with 47% of reviewers giving five stars and 19% giving one. LanguaTalk's review, updated in June 2026, lands in the middle at 3 out of 5.</p>

  <p>On Trustpilot, the praise is for engaging lessons people can customise, real speaking practice, and a judgment-free place to make mistakes. LanguaTalk adds natural-feeling conversations, a clean interface and guided questions for beginners, and recommends Praktika for beginners and lower-intermediate learners who want structure.</p>

  <p>The complaints repeat as well. Trustpilot reviewers report speech recognition that mistakes which language you're speaking, lag and freezes mid-lesson, a conversation-first start that overwhelms some beginners, and, repeatedly, charges after the trial ended and refunds that were refused. LanguaTalk adds that learning paths are rigid (changing your goal resets your progress) and that saved words never come back for review.</p>

  <p>My verdict, from its screens and the reviews, is that Praktika is a good guided conversation course and a weak tool for two specific jobs, keeping the vocabulary you meet and proving your accent is improving. If those two jobs are why you're shopping, the rest of this page is for you. If they aren't, Praktika is probably the cheaper right answer.</p>

  <h2>What Praktika does well</h2>

  <p>Praktika's core loop is a spoken conversation with an animated AI avatar. Its App Store listing says "Each tutor has a distinct personality and accent (American, British, Latin American, and more) and adapts to your level, pace, and goals," and that the tutor can explain things in the language you already speak. For a beginner, talking to a face lowers the barrier in a way a chat bubble doesn't.</p>

  <p>The library is enormous. The listing counts over a thousand lessons covering "travel, careers, job interviews, business, everyday conversation, slang, and IELTS and TOEFL prep," plus a multimodal feature that builds lessons from a photo, a PDF, a video or a voice note you upload. It names thirteen languages: English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Portuguese (European and Brazilian), Japanese, Korean, Chinese, Arabic, Russian, Polish and Dutch. It runs on iOS, Android and the web.</p>

  <p>And it's cheap. Praktika's own site says roughly $8 a month, against its estimate of $400 a month for a private tutor. The US App Store lists Premium plans from $6.99 to $139.99, including a $9.99 one-month option, though LanguaTalk and PracticeMe both only saw 3-month and annual plans inside the app. There's a free tier with limits, and PracticeMe reports a 7-day trial that asks for payment details up front.</p>

  <h2>Where Praktika falls short</h2>

  <p>Two gaps matter if you're comparing.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No end-of-lesson summary.</strong> The listing promises "pronunciation and fluency feedback" after every conversation, but LanguaTalk and PracticeMe both found no end-of-lesson summary of what you got wrong. Corrections stay in the chat flow, and you scroll back to find them. PracticeMe adds that conversations follow a script, and wandering off-topic gets pulled back to the lesson.</li>
    <li><strong>No per-sound history that either review mentions.</strong> PracticeMe describes a 2025 upgrade to pronunciation feedback, and says a language teacher found the "British" tutor spoke with an American accent, which Praktika's chief product officer said on Reddit had been fixed. Neither review describes a way to see whether a specific sound is improving month over month.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>None of these matter much if what you want is a guided course. They matter a lot if what you want is to fix one accent problem and prove to yourself it's fixed.</p>

  <h2>What Vocele does differently</h2>

  <p>The honest minuses first. Vocele has no avatars; its coaches, Luna and Lorenzo, are voices with names. It teaches nine languages: Spanish, English, French, German, Italian, Portuguese, Japanese, Russian and Polish. It's iPhone only. And it costs more, starting at $14.99 a month. What you get for that is review and tracking around the conversation.</p>

  <h3>Corrections inside your own sentence</h3>

  <p>When you say "I have five years experience in marketing," Vocele highlights the gap in the bubble you just spoke and puts the fix beneath it: "five years <strong>of</strong> experience." It doesn't reply with a rewritten paragraph. I chose that over a separate correction card because a card tells you the right sentence and the highlight tells you where you broke yours. Where is what you remember. There's also a corrections-only mode that stops the coach explaining and just fixes.</p>

  <h3>Per-word pronunciation scores, with history</h3>

  <p>Every word you say in accent practice gets a score, and Vocele names the sound that dragged a low one down. I'll admit the number is the less important half. What I care about is your score history, week after week, going up. A single score is a verdict. A trend is a coach. The <a href="/blog/ai-accent-training">accent training explainer</a> covers how the scoring works.</p>

  <h3>A word-learning system, which Praktika doesn't have</h3>

  <p>This is the gap LanguaTalk names on Praktika's side, and it's where Vocele is strongest. Vocabulary runs on spaced repetition, so a word from Tuesday's conversation returns on Friday, before you'd forget it, across six practice modes: flashcards, matching, typing, listening, picture and cloze rounds. Themed word sets cover the situations you're actually in, such as work, travel, the doctor and your kid's school. Any phrase from a chat, including a correction, can be saved into a phrasebook with its own review schedule. Grammar rules are explained in your own language, with reports of sentences where you could have used each rule.</p>

  <h3>Stories you answer out loud</h3>

  <p>Reading isn't a separate silent module. Short scenes are tuned to your CEFR level, read aloud to you, and then you answer comprehension questions by speaking. You retell the same scene from another character's point of view, and the story comes back days later so it sticks.</p>

  <h3>A plan built from your errors</h3>

  <p>You pick interests, and each day's plan is built from yesterday's errors. Conversations are open-topic, so if the thing on your mind is Thursday's job interview, you talk about Thursday's job interview.</p>

