  <h2>Which one should you pick</h2>

  <p><strong>Pick Praktika if</strong> you're a beginner who wants a guided path, you learn better talking to a face, you need Korean, Chinese, Arabic or Dutch, or price decides it. It's a very good app for that.</p>

  <p><strong>Pick Vocele if</strong> you already understand a lot and your problem is output, because you freeze or people ask you to repeat yourself. If you want vocabulary that actually stays learned instead of fading after the lesson. If you want to see, in numbers, that your accent is changing. And if you're on iPhone.</p>

  <p><strong>Pick neither if</strong> you need a human. Both apps cost far less than a private tutor at $200 to $320 a month for two lessons a week, and I've written up <a href="/blog/ai-speaking-coach-vs-human-tutor">where an AI coach loses to a tutor</a>. Some people need the accountability of a person. And if your language isn't on either list, TalkPal covers more than 130; <a href="/vs/talkpal">Vocele vs TalkPal</a> compares the two honestly.</p>

  <h2>A note on our own gaps</h2>

  <p>When I audited what Praktika, Fluently, Speak and ELSA had that Vocele didn't, five of the six gaps on my list turned out to be already shipped or stale. Lists built from marketing copy overstate gaps, and that applies to this table too. Treat the table above as what each app does today, from its store listing and the reviews cited, and expect it to change. ELSA got its own page later: <a href="/vs/elsa">Vocele vs ELSA Speak</a> compares the pronunciation-first app.</p>

  <p>Vocele isn't on the App Store yet. If the per-word scoring is what you came for, <a href="/pricing">see the plans and the 3-day trial</a>; this page will be updated when the App Store listing goes live.</p>
